---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const ModelPlan = `Program: Study Break Picker
What it does: you say how many minutes you have,
and it prints the breaks that fit.

1. Make a list called activities holding break names.
2. Make a list called times holding how long each one
   takes, in the same order.
3. Write a procedure fitsIn(limit):
   a. Print a heading that includes limit.
   b. Set a counter called found to 0.
   c. Loop over every position in the two lists.
      i. If the time at that position is less than or
         equal to limit, print the name and the time,
         and add 1 to found.
   d. If found is still 0, print "Nothing fits."
4. Ask the user how many minutes they have.
5. Call fitsIn with that number.`;

const OverScopedPlan = `Program: Study Planner
What it does: keeps track of all my classes and
assignments and tells me what to work on.

1. Let the user add a class.
2. Let the user add an assignment with a due date.
3. Save everything so it is still there tomorrow.
4. Show a calendar for the week.
5. Sort the assignments by how urgent they are.
6. Let the user check things off.`;

const ReqFourShape = `function fitsIn(limit)
{
  console.log("Breaks under " + limit + " minutes:");
  let found = 0;

  for (let i = 0; i < activities.length; i++)
  {
    if (times[i] <= limit)
    {
      // ... print it, and count it
    }
  }

  if (found === 0)
  {
    // ... say nothing fits
  }
}`;

const ReqFourFail = `let activities = ["Stretch", "Walk outside", "Power nap"];
let times = [5, 15, 25];
let limit = Number(prompt("How many minutes? "));

let picks = [];

for (let i = 0; i < activities.length; i++)
{
  if (times[i] <= limit)
  {
    picks.push(activities[i]);
  }
}

printAll(picks);

function printAll(list)
{
  console.log("You could: " + list);
}`;

const GateForm = `Program: <name>
What it does: <one sentence>

Input:      <what you ask for>
List:       <its name, and what is in it>
Procedure:  <its name, and its parameter>
Inside it:  <the loop, and the decision>

Steps:
1. ...
2. ...`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Build 1: Your First Project</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- The Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Brief</h2>

          <p>
            Two sessions. <b>You choose what to make.</b> Terminal or p5 &mdash;
            your call.
          </p>

          <p>
            Everything you need has been in your hands since last session.
            Quarter 1 gave you input, lists, decisions, and loops. The last seven
            activities added the parameterized procedure. <b>That is the complete
            set</b> &mdash; this is the first point in the course where a real
            project is possible, so we are going to build one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>The scope rule: a person could use the finished thing in under
            two minutes.</b> This is a rule, not a suggestion. A plan that breaks
            it does not get approved.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>No video. No written reference.</b> Those belong to Build 2 and to
            the Create task. This one is just the program.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Build 1</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Your First Project</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Scoping a Project</p>
              <p>The Four Requirements</p>
              <p>The One That Fails Silently</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>You Have Everything</h2>
              <p>Input. Lists. Decisions. Loops.</p>
              <p class="fragment">And now a procedure with a parameter.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                That is every ingredient the Create task asks for.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Four Requirements</h2>
              <ol>
                <li class="fragment">Input</li>
                <li class="fragment">A list the program actually needs</li>
                <li class="fragment">
                  A procedure of yours with a parameter that changes what it does
                </li>
                <li class="fragment">
                  Sequencing, selection, and iteration <b>inside that
                  procedure</b>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Read Number Four Again</h2>
              <p>Not somewhere in your program.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <b>Inside the body of the procedure you wrote.</b>
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                This is the requirement people lose points on without ever
                noticing.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>If You Build in p5</h2>
              <p>
                <code>setup()</code>, <code>draw()</code>, and{" "}
                <code>mousePressed()</code> are <b>not</b> yours.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">You need one alongside them that is.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Scope Rule</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">
                Someone could use the finished thing in under two minutes.
              </h3>
              <p class="fragment">Not "it takes two minutes to write."</p>
              <p class="fragment">Two minutes to <i>use</i>.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why That Rule Exists</h2>
              <p>Scoping badly is the most common way the Create task goes wrong.</p>
              <p class="fragment">It is not something you can be told.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                So we are going to get it wrong here, where it costs two sessions
                instead of eight.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today</h2>
              <p><b>Twenty minutes:</b> write your plan.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <b>No code until it is approved.</b>
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">Then build.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Next Session</h2>
              <p>Build. Then read your plan out loud.</p>
              <p class="fragment">And tell us what actually happened.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Scope</dt>
                  <dd>How much a project is trying to do.</dd>
                  <dt>Prose pseudocode</dt>
                  <dd>
                    Numbered plain-English steps, detailed enough that someone
                    else could write the code from them.
                  </dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Requirements -->
        <section id="requirements">
          <h2>The Four Requirements</h2>

          <p>
            These are four of the six the Create task will ask for in Quarter 3.
            They are the same words.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Input.</b> Your program asks for something and uses the answer.
              In the terminal that is <code>prompt()</code>. In p5 it is{" "}
              <code>mousePressed()</code> with <code>mouseX</code> and{" "}
              <code>mouseY</code>.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>A list your program actually needs.</b> Not a list you added so
              there would be one. If deleting it would not break anything, it
              does not count.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>A procedure you wrote, with a parameter that changes what it
              does.</b> The test: pass a different argument, and something
              different should happen. If you have to edit the body to change the
              result, the parameter is decorative.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Sequencing, selection, and iteration inside that procedure's
              body.</b> Statements in order, an <code>if</code>, and a loop &mdash;
              all three between that procedure's braces.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            Two more come free once you have those four: your procedure gets{" "}
            <b>called</b>, and your program produces <b>output based on the
            input</b>. Check them anyway.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Requirement 4 -->
        <section id="requirement-four">
          <h2>The One That Fails Silently</h2>

          <p>
            Requirement 4 is the only one you can satisfy and still lose the
            point. Here is the shape it wants &mdash; bodies left out on purpose,
            because the program is yours to write.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={ReqFourShape}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="All three, inside one procedure"
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            Statements run in order, there is a loop, and there is a decision.
            All three are between the braces of a procedure that takes a
            parameter.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>And here is the version that fails</h3>

          <Code
            code={ReqFourFail}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Everything in the right program, in the wrong place"
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            This program has input, a list, a loop, a decision, and a procedure
            with a parameter. It runs, and it prints{" "}
            <code>You could: Stretch,Walk outside</code>. <b>It still fails
            requirement 4</b>, because the loop and the decision are outside{" "}
            <code>printAll()</code>. Between that procedure's braces there is one
            line, and it is neither.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix is not to add anything. It is to <b>move the loop and the{" "}
            <code>if</code> inside the procedure</b> and pass{" "}
            <code>limit</code> as the parameter. Same program, same output, and
            requirement 4 now holds.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Before you submit, do this one check.</b> Find your procedure. Put
            your finger on its opening brace and its closing brace. Between those
            two marks, can you point at a statement, a loop, and an{" "}
            <code>if</code>? If not, restructure now &mdash; it is much cheaper
            today than it will be in Quarter 3.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Ideas -->
        <section id="ideas">
          <h2>Ideas</h2>

          <p>
            You do not have to use one of these. They exist to show the size we
            are aiming for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Terminal</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>Movie picker.</b> You say how many minutes you have; it prints
              the films from a list that fit.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Packing list.</b> You say how many days the trip is; it prints
              what to bring and how many.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Five-question quiz.</b> Questions in a list, asked one at a
              time, scored at the end.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Playlist filter.</b> Songs with a mood attached; you pick a
              mood, it prints the matches.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Team splitter.</b> A list of names and a number of teams; it
              deals them out.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Locker assigner.</b> A list of names; it prints who gets which
              locker, skipping the ones that are broken.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="mt-4">p5</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <b>Sticker stamper.</b> A list of shapes; each click places the
              next one.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Palette painter.</b> A list of colors; clicking cycles through
              them.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Constellation.</b> Each click adds a star to a list, and the
              sketch connects them in order.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Target practice.</b> A list of target positions; clicking near
              one removes it.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p class="text-body-secondary mt-3">
            Every one of these passes the two-minute rule, and every one of them
            has an obvious list and an obvious procedure. That is not an
            accident &mdash; it is what a well-scoped project looks like.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Session 34 -->
        <section id="gate">
          <h2>Session 34: The Scoping Gate</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Twenty minutes, and no code until your plan is approved.</b>
          </p>

          <p>
            Write your plan in <b>prose pseudocode</b> &mdash; numbered
            plain-English steps, detailed enough that someone else could write
            the program from them. Not JavaScript, and not exam notation either.
            Plain sentences, in order.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={GateForm}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="What to hand me"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <h3 class="mt-5">A plan that gets approved</h3>

          <Code
            code={ModelPlan}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Study Break Picker"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            Read step 3 again. The procedure has a parameter, and inside its body
            there is a heading and a counter (sequencing), a loop over the list
            (iteration), and two decisions (selection). <b>All four requirements
            are visible in the plan</b>, before a single line is written. That is
            what the gate is checking for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">A plan that does not</h3>

          <Code
            code={OverScopedPlan}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Study Planner"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            This is a good idea and a bad plan. Step 3 is not possible with
            anything you know. Steps 1, 2, and 6 each need their own input loop.
            And nobody uses it in under two minutes &mdash; adding your classes
            alone takes longer than that.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>The tell is that it has no procedure and no parameter anywhere in
            it.</b> A plan that cannot name those two things is not finished,
            however good the idea is.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            If your plan gets sent back, that is the system working. The whole
            reason Build 1 is two sessions long is so that finding this out is
            cheap.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Session 35 -->
        <section id="share-out">
          <h2>Session 35: Build, Then Share Out</h2>

          <p>Build for most of the session. Then, in the last fifteen minutes:</p>

          <ol>
            <li><b>Read your session 34 plan out loud.</b> The one you wrote, not a tidied version.</li>
            <li><b>Say what actually happened.</b> What did you cut? What took longer than you thought? What turned out to be easy?</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b>This is the point of Build 1.</b> Most plans will have been too
            ambitious. Finding that out now, out loud, in a room where everyone
            else is finding out the same thing, is worth more than the program
            is. Session 46 asks you to scope a six-session project, and this is
            the experience you will be held to when you do.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Unfinished at the end of the session? Finish it at home. Points 1
            through 5 on the rubric do not care when it was finished.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Rubric -->
        <section id="rubric">
          <h2>Rubric</h2>

          <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>What</th>
                  <th class="text-end">Points</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Scoping plan written in prose pseudocode and approved at the gate</td>
                  <td class="text-end">8</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Input &mdash; the program asks for something and uses the answer</td>
                  <td class="text-end">4</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>A list the program actually needs</td>
                  <td class="text-end">6</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>A procedure you wrote, with a parameter that changes what it does</td>
                  <td class="text-end">8</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>
                    <b>Sequencing, selection, and iteration inside that procedure's body</b>
                  </td>
                  <td class="text-end">8</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Share-out &mdash; read your plan, said what actually happened</td>
                  <td class="text-end">6</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td class="fw-bold">Total</td>
                  <td class="text-end fw-bold">40</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Notice what is not on this list: how ambitious the idea was, and
            whether everything you planned got finished. A project that got cut
            in half and works loses nothing here.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Three things go to the dropbox:</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Your <b>session 34 plan</b>, as written on the day.</li>
            <li>Your <b>program</b>.</li>
            <li>
              One line naming <b>your procedure and its parameter</b>, so I know
              which one to check requirement 4 against.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Build Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
